This page is the second 'récapitulation' of the setup used in the Teichmüller notes. It fixes the base-point formalism that the next pages turn into path torsors.

**Setup.** Let k be an algebraically closed field of characteristic 0, so there is a canonical map Q → k. Let X be a curve of type (g,ν) over k, meaning a smooth genus-g curve with ν punctures, and let x ∈ X be a point. The pair (X,x) determines:
- a k-point ξ of the moduli stack M_{g,ν}, so that X is the fibre (𝔛_{g,ν,k})_ξ of the universal curve;
- a k-point x of the universal curve 𝔛_{g,ν}.

Since 𝔛_{g,ν} with the marked sections removed is M_{g,ν+1}, the universal curve is essentially M_{g,ν+1} → M_{g,ν}.

Via Q → k, the points ξ and x are geometric points of M_{g,ν,Q} and 𝔛_{g,ν,Q}. Diagram (0) is the cartesian ladder of the base change from Q to k: the universal curve over the moduli stack over Spec Q, and the same over Spec k.

**Diagram (1).** Taking étale fundamental groups (of stacks) at these base points gives a 3×3 diagram of exact sequences with these defined groups (2):
- π_{X,x} = π_1(X,x), the profinite surface group;
- 𝔊_{X,x} = π_1(𝔛_{g,ν,k}, x), the geometric fundamental group of the universal curve, ≅ \hat Γ_{g,ν+1};
- 𝔗_X = π_1(M_{g,ν,k}, ξ), the geometric profinite Teichmüller (mapping class) group;
- M_{X,x} = π_1(𝔛_{g,ν,Q}, x), arithmetic;
- N_X = π_1(M_{g,ν,Q}, ξ), arithmetic.

The diagram consists of:
- columns 1 → π → 𝔊 → 𝔗 → 1 and 1 → π → M → N → 1, i.e. the profinite Birman sequences coming from the homotopy exact sequence of the curve fibration;
- rows 1 → 𝔊 → M → Γ_{Q̃/Q} → 1 and 1 → 𝔗 → N → Γ_{Q̃/Q} → 1, i.e. geometric versus arithmetic fundamental groups.

Here Q̃ is the algebraic closure of Q inside k (marginal note), and the Galois column is an equality. The NB restates the diagram as the filtration π ⊂ 𝔊 ⊂ M of normal subgroups, with quotients M/π = N, M/𝔊 = Γ, and N/𝔗 = Γ.

**Comparison objects.** Each group is non-canonically isomorphic to a 'standard' object: \hat π_{g,ν}, \hat 𝔊_{g,ν}, \hat 𝔗_{g,ν}, M_{g,ν}, N_{g,ν}. These are defined the same way from a complex pair (X_0,x_0) of type (g,ν), which gives geometric points ξ_0, x_0 of the moduli stacks over Q. The standard geometric groups are then profinite completions of the discrete topological groups (surface group, mapping class groups Γ_{g,ν+1} and Γ_{g,ν}), via Riemann existence.

**Purpose.** The next page introduces the torsor P_{X,x} under M_{g,ν}: étale paths from (x_0,ξ_0) to (x,ξ). Every group in (1) is then a contracted product of the standard diagram (1_{g,ν}) with this torsor. The previous page uses a complex embedding φ to reduce the structure group to the discrete Teichmüller group ('discrétification arithmétique'). So this page is the base-point and groupoid bookkeeping for that programme: the Galois action on the tower of profinite Teichmüller groups.
